{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01AfDoVsjyi51fFzzjkCmp6B","result":"---\nname: generate-context-pack\ndescription: >\n  Generates normative context-pack markdown files from a structured analysis\n  document. Each generated file carries a `when_to_read` routing-frontmatter block\n  and follows the mandatory context-pack format (Purpose, Scope, Mandatory Rules,\n  Recommended Rules, Restrictions, Examples, Exceptions, Traceability, Version).\n  Activate when the task mentions \"generate context pack\", \"context-pack\n  generation\", or references a project root with analysis outputs ready.\nmetadata:\n  author: aipods-team\n  version: 2.2.0\n  category: context-pack\n  tags: context-pack, generation, onboarding, agent-native, when-to-read\n---\n\n# Generate Context Pack\n\nReads `artifacts/outputs/context-pack-analysis/analysis.md` (produced by\n`analyze-context-pack-inputs`) and writes the standard set of normative context-pack\nfiles into `./context-pack/`. Each generated file opens with a `when_to_read` frontmatter\nblock so the Stepwise harness can surface, in the CONTEXT-PACK manifest, *when* a coding\nagent should load that file — before it decides to open it. **All content traces to\n`analysis.md`; this skill never invents rules, constraints, or domain facts.**\n\n## Generation shape & protocol route\n\n**Shape: list-shape** — many discrete files, one written and flushed at a time (each file\nis short and multi-section; there is no single consolidated spec, so §10's section-by-\nsection protocol does not apply). Apply `execution-protocol.md` in this order:\n\n`§2` (FIRST/LAST `_progress.json`, list-shape variant) → **`§14` (Parallel fan-out —\ncapability check, see below)** → `§10.5` (tool discipline incl. §10.5.1 non-ASCII fallback —\napplies to every file write) → `§11` (harness output sidecar). Also: `§5` (FIC monitoring),\n`§7` (REPAIR when `failure_feedback` present), `§9` (non-interactive), `§4` (Memory Bank —\nthis skill opts in).\n\n`execution-protocol.md` owns those mechanics. This SKILL.md supplies the skill-specific\nbits: the file catalog, the mandatory file format, and the `when_to_read` frontmatter\ncontract below.\n\n**Where the protocol is — do not search for it.** Stepwise stages it at\n`{base_path}/context-pack/system/`:\n\n```\n{base_path}/context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md              # routing rule + §2, §10.5, §11 (universal, inline)\n{base_path}/context-pack/system/execution-protocol/<NN>-<name>.md  # every other section, one file each\n```\n\nResolve each `§N` in the route above to its file via that directory listing, and read **only**\nthe sections your route selects — this skill is list-shape, so §10 (section generation) is not\nin its route at all. Reading the ~44 KB root file end-to-end to find a section that lives in\nits own file is wasted context; the routing table it exists to serve is in its first 60 lines.\nIf the directory is genuinely absent, note it and proceed — do not sweep the filesystem.\n\n### §14 parallel fan-out — capability check\n\nThis skill is a declared §14 unit set: **one unit per normative context-pack file**, and the\nunits are independent (no cross-file dependencies except `context-pack-index.md`, which is\nalways written last and serially).\n\n**Fan out whenever the harness can dispatch multiple workers in a single turn** — concurrent\nforeground workers alone qualify; background/detached tasks are NOT required, and an absent\n`Parallel delegation:` advertisement does NOT force inline (execution-protocol.md §14\nApplicability). Dispatch one worker per target file, and **emit all workers for the shard in\nONE turn** (§14.3 step 2) — one worker per turn serializes them.\n\n**Fall back to inline — every unit in catalog order, per Step 2 — ONLY when the harness is\ngenuinely single-dispatch.** This is a normal execution path, not a failure; the output is\nidentical whether produced in parallel or inline.\n\n**Fan-out does not relax any other rule.** Each worker still writes exactly one file with the\nfull mandatory format and `when_to_read` frontmatter, and the coordinator still owns\n`_progress.json` (Step 2.5) and writes `context-pack-index.md` last.\n\n## Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Type | Required | Default | Description |\n|-----------|------|----------|---------|-------------|\n| `base_path` | string | No | `./` | Project root. |\n| `target_files` | string | No | `all` | `all`, or a comma-separated subset of catalog filenames. |\n| `failure_feedback` | string | No | — | REPAIR-mode directives (triggers §7). |\n\n**STOP-GATE — abort before any generation if:**\n- `{base_path}/artifacts/outputs/context-pack-analysis/analysis.md` does not exist — run\n  `analyze-context-pack-inputs` first. Write a Gap Report and EXIT per §9.\n\n## Path resolution\n\n```\nINPUT_ANALYSIS  = {base_path}/artifacts/outputs/context-pack-analysis/\nOUTPUT_PACK     = {base_path}/context-pack/            # generated files land here\nPROGRESS_FOLDER = {base_path}/artifacts/outputs/context-pack-generation/   # _progress.json, audit, recovery\n```\n\nCreate `OUTPUT_PACK` and `PROGRESS_FOLDER` if missing. **NEVER write to\n`OUTPUT_PACK/system/`** — that directory is owned by the Stepwise registry installer\n(`execution-protocol.md`, `active-context.md`, `progress.md`). Generated files go in the\n`OUTPUT_PACK` root only. Keep `_progress.json` in `PROGRESS_FOLDER`, never in `OUTPUT_PACK`\n(a stray file there would pollute the context-pack manifest the harness scans).\n\n## Session identity\n\nDerive `SESSION_ID` per `execution-protocol.md §1` with TYPE prefix `CTXPACK`. REPAIR\nreuses the prior `SESSION_ID` from the existing audit filename. Do NOT construct it from a\nliteral date/UUID formula here.\n\n## Zero Invention Policy\n\nEvery rule, restriction, and example MUST derive from `analysis.md` (or\n`meeting-analysis.md` when present) — not from the model's general knowledge:\n\n- No source evidence for a file → write the mandatory skeleton with `[TO BE COMPLETED]`\n  markers and a `status: pending — insufficient evidence` note in the body. Never\n  fabricate rules to fill the template.\n- Vague filler (\"use best practices\") is forbidden — every rule must be specific and\n  actionable, traceable to an `analysis.md` line via `## Traceability`.\n- Inferred hints are allowed ONLY tagged `[INFERRED]` and only in `## Recommended Rules`,\n  never in `## Mandatory Rules`.\n- **Carry `supersedes …` clauses through to `## Traceability`.** `analysis.md` has already\n  resolved every project-input-vs-blueprint conflict in the project's favour (see\n  `analyze-context-pack-inputs` § Source Precedence), so the winning value is the only one you\n  will find — write that rule and only that rule. But when a bullet's provenance names a\n  superseded blueprint mandate, record it in `## Traceability`: the next reader needs to know\n  the deviation was a decision, not an oversight, or they will \"fix\" the pack back to the\n  blueprint default.\n\n## The `when_to_read` frontmatter contract (REQUIRED on every generated file)\n\nEvery generated context-pack file MUST begin with a YAML frontmatter block whose\n`when_to_read` key states, in 1–3 concrete lines, the situations in which a coding agent\nshould load this file *before acting*. The Stepwise loader parses this key and renders it\nas a `Read when:` line in the CONTEXT-PACK manifest, so the agent sees the trigger before\ndeciding to open the file.\n\n```markdown\n---\nwhen_to_read: |\n  <Specific, actionable triggers — the concrete tasks/situations where THIS file is\n  load-bearing. Name the activities, not the topic.>\n---\n\n# <Title>\n...\n```\n\nRules for `when_to_read`:\n- **Derive triggers from what the file governs** (see the per-file triggers in\n  `references/file-catalog.md`) — do not invent, and do not copy the file's `## Purpose`\n  verbatim. Purpose = *what it is*; `when_to_read` = *when to open it*.\n- Keep it specific and imperative (\"Read when implementing any new endpoint, writing use\n  cases, adding controllers/adapters…\"), not a vague topic label.\n- Use a YAML `|` block scalar; the loader collapses interior whitespace into one manifest\n  line, so keep it to a few lines.\n- **Routing files** — `context-pack-index.md`, `project-inventory.md`, `codebase-map.md`, `ux-design.md` —\n  are read-first indexes. For these four, ALSO make the body's `## Purpose` first line\n  begin with `ROUTING:` so the harness marks them `→ ROUTING (read first)` in the manifest.\n  Non-routing files keep a normal `## Purpose`.\n- Emit `when_to_read` even under `[TO BE COMPLETED]` — the routing hint is valid regardless\n  of body completeness.\n\n## Mandatory file format\n\n```markdown\n---\nwhen_to_read: |\n  <triggers, per the contract above>\n---\n\n# <Title>\n\n## Purpose\n<What decision or behavior this file governs. Prefix with `ROUTING:` for the four routing files.>\n\n## Scope\n<Teams, repositories, layers, or project areas this applies to>\n\n## Mandatory Rules\n- <Specific, actionable rule derived from analysis.md>\n\n## Recommended Rules\n- <Recommended practice; `[INFERRED]` allowed here only>\n\n## Restrictions and Prohibitions\n- <What is explicitly prohibited>\n\n## Examples\n- Valid: <example>\n- Invalid: <example>\n\n## Exceptions\n- <When deviation is allowed and who approves>\n\n## Traceability\n- <analysis.md sections / source documents each rule derives from>\n\n## Version and Owner\n- Version: 1.0\n- Owner: <derived from source material or \"Project Team\">\n```\n\n## Workflow\n\n### FIRST ACTION — MANDATORY (§2)\n\nBefore any other file write, write `_progress.json` (list-shape variant: `total`,\n`completed`, `items[]`) to `PROGRESS_FOLDER`, `\"session_id\": \"initializing\"`. Set `total`\nto the number of target files once known. This prevents orchestrator SIGINT.\n\nLoad the Memory Bank per §4 Session Start (read `./context-pack/active-context.md` /\n`progress.md` if present; write `active-context.md` with status `STARTING`).\n\n### Step 1 — Load analysis\n\nRead `references/phase-a-analysis-loading.md`. Read every file in `INPUT_ANALYSIS`\n(`analysis.md`, plus `meeting-analysis.md` and `transcripts/` if present). Determine the\ntarget file set from `target_files` (default: the full catalog in\n`references/file-catalog.md`).\n\n### Step 2 — Generate each file (Write-Flush-Forget, P1)\n\nRead `references/phase-b-file-generation.md` and `references/file-catalog.md`.\n\n**Order is the catalog order in `references/file-catalog.md`, not alphabetical.** The catalog\nbegins `domain.md` → `arch-standards.md` → `tech-policy.md` → `coding-standards.md` → … and\nends with `context-pack-index.md`. Emitting files alphabetically is a deviation: it reorders\nthe run without changing the output set, which makes an interrupted run harder to reason about\nagainst `items[]`. Under §14 fan-out the same order governs dispatch.\n\n**Triage before dispatch — separate the skeleton files from the real ones.** Read the\n`analysis.md` sections that feed each target file and split the set in two:\n\n- **Zero-evidence files** — every feeding section is `status: pending` with no `complete` item.\n  Per the Zero Invention Policy these have exactly one correct body: `when_to_read` frontmatter\n  + the mandatory skeleton with `[TO BE COMPLETED]` markers and a\n  `status: pending — insufficient evidence` note. There is no content decision to make. Assign\n  **all of them to a single worker** (or write them in one inline pass), not one worker each.\n- **Evidence-bearing files** — one §14 worker each, as above.\n\nThis changes no output byte: the skeleton text is fixed by the format contract either way. It\nexists because a thin analysis can make most of the catalog zero-evidence, and dispatching a\nworker per fixed string spends a full inference round-trip to decide nothing. Never batch an\nevidence-bearing file to save a dispatch — those carry real per-file judgment.\n\nFor EACH target file, in catalog order — **do not stop; process ALL target files:**\n\n1. **Load** only the `analysis.md` section(s) that feed this file (per the catalog's\n   `Source:` mapping). Do not carry other files' content in working memory.\n2. **Source fidelity check (pre-write gate):** every Mandatory Rule must map to specific\n   analysis evidence; if evidence is insufficient, switch this file to the\n   `[TO BE COMPLETED]` skeleton path. No rule without a traceable source.\n3. **Compose** the file: `when_to_read` frontmatter (per the contract) + the mandatory\n   format body. Set the `## Purpose` `ROUTING:` prefix for the four routing files.\n4. **Write** the file to `OUTPUT_PACK/{filename}` — MANDATORY TOOL CALL, do not defer.\n   Use `Write` (full file); never `Bash`/`sed`/`cat >` to compose it (§10.5). Under\n   non-ASCII content, `Write` is already the full-file path (§10.5.1) — safe.\n5. **Verify** the file exists and is non-empty, then **immediately update `_progress.json`** —\n   `completed += 1` and append the filename to `items[]`. **This is a mandatory tool call in\n   the same turn as the write, not a deferred bookkeeping step.** On the first update also flip\n   `session_id` from `\"initializing\"` to the derived `SESSION_ID` (§1) and confirm `total`\n   equals the target-file count.\n\n   **Why this is non-negotiable:** `_progress.json` is the only durable record of what\n   completed. A run that writes files without updating it reports `completed: 0` while the\n   pack is half-built, and an interrupt at that point leaves REPAIR with no knowledge of the\n   finished files — it redoes all of them. Files on disk are *not* a substitute: the pack\n   directory legitimately contains files from earlier runs.\n\n   Under §14 fan-out the **coordinator** performs this update as each worker returns; workers\n   never write `_progress.json` concurrently.\n6. **Flush** the file content from working memory before the next file.\n\n### Step 3 — Write the index last\n\nGenerate `context-pack-index.md` last (it links every produced file). It is a routing\nfile: `when_to_read` frontmatter + `## Purpose` starting with `ROUTING:`.\n\n### REPAIR mode (§7)\n\nIf a `## ⚠️ Repair feedback` block is present: identify the target file(s) from the\nfeedback, regenerate ONLY those (re-running steps 1–6 for each), preserve all other files\nverbatim, bump the affected files' `Version`, and record the delta in the audit. Do NOT\nregenerate the whole pack.\n\n### LAST ACTION — MANDATORY (§2, §4, §11)\n\nIn this strict order (§11.4):\n1. Verify every target file exists, is non-empty, and opens with `when_to_read`\n   frontmatter (spot-check the four routing files for the `ROUTING:` purpose prefix).\n1a. **Reconcile `_progress.json` against disk.** `completed` must equal `total`, `items[]` must\n   list every generated filename, and `session_id` must not be `\"initializing\"`. A mismatch\n   means Step 2.5 was skipped during the run: repair the file to reflect what is actually on\n   disk and record the discrepancy in the audit. Never flip to `COMPLETED` over a\n   `completed: 0` record — that is the state an interrupted run cannot recover from.\n2. Memory Bank session-end (§4): overwrite `./context-pack/active-context.md`; append one\n   `progress.md` milestone row. **Artifact type: `context-pack-files`** (e.g.\n   `17 context-pack-files`).\n3. Flip `_progress.json` to `COMPLETED`.\n4. **Harness output sidecar (§11)** — apply `execution-protocol.md §11` when the prompt\n   has a `## Run metadata` block with `output_file`. **Output parameter this skill\n   produces** (one key per row of the prompt's `## Output parameters` table):\n   - `context_pack_path`: the `OUTPUT_PACK` directory path (`./context-pack/`).\n\n   Example sidecar contents (illustrative):\n   ```json\n   { \"context_pack_path\": \"./context-pack\" }\n   ```\n5. `final_response` — no tool calls after the sidecar write.\n\n## Error handling\n\n- `analysis.md` missing → STOP-GATE (Gap Report + EXIT).\n- Insufficient evidence for a file → `[TO BE COMPLETED]` skeleton with a `pending` note;\n  still emit valid `when_to_read` frontmatter.\n- A single file write fails → log, retry once; on repeated failure record it in\n  `_progress.json` and the audit and continue with remaining files.\n\n## Reference files\n\n- `references/phase-a-analysis-loading.md` — reading `analysis.md`, target-file selection, the source-fidelity gate.\n- `references/phase-b-file-generation.md` — per-file generation loop, the mandatory format, and the full `when_to_read` + `ROUTING:` authoring rules with examples.\n- `references/file-catalog.md` — the standard file catalog: what each file governs, its `analysis.md` source mapping, and its per-file `when_to_read` triggers.\n\n## Related skills\n\n- `analyze-context-pack-inputs` — upstream producer of `analysis.md` (the sole input).\n- `create-capability` / capability YAMLs — downstream consumers reference the context pack via `context_pack.location`; the `when_to_read` frontmatter drives the CONTEXT-PACK manifest the executor sees.\n","isError":false}}
