{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01MWjwCtQrLb3MoKNegsXoCf","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/.coda/skills/generate-context-pack/references/phase-b-file-generation.md\" lines=\"168\" start=\"1\" end=\"168\" returned=\"168\" has_more=\"false\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Phase B — Per-File Generation & `when_to_read` Frontmatter\n     2|\n     3|## Context Contract\n     4|\n     5|- **H1 Title:** Phase B — Per-File Generation & `when_to_read` Frontmatter\n     6|- **Inputs:** `TARGET_FILES[]`, `SOURCE_MAP`, `EVIDENCE_INDEX` (from Phase A); `analysis.md` section(s) read lazily per file; `references/file-catalog.md`.\n     7|- **Outputs:** one normative markdown file per target in `OUTPUT_PACK/`, each opening with a `when_to_read` frontmatter block; `context-pack-index.md` last.\n     8|- **Carries Forward:** `_progress.json.items[]` (filenames written) — nothing else. Each file's body is flushed after write.\n     9|- **Flush After:** every file's composed content, immediately after its verified write (Write-Flush-Forget, P1).\n    10|- **Dependency:** runs after Phase A; each file write is gated by the source-fidelity check (Phase A Step A4).\n    11|- **Used by step:** SKILL.md Step 2 and Step 3.\n    12|\n    13|## Mode-specific behavior\n    14|\n    15|- **BUILD:** loop over all `TARGET_FILES` in catalog order; index last.\n    16|- **REPAIR (§7):** regenerate ONLY the file(s) named in the feedback; preserve every other file verbatim; bump the regenerated file's `Version`; record the delta in the audit.\n    17|\n    18|## Per-file loop (Write-Flush-Forget)\n    19|\n    20|For EACH target file — **do not stop; process ALL target files:**\n    21|\n    22|1. **Load** only this file's `SOURCE_MAP` section(s) from `analysis.md`. Do not carry other files' content forward.\n    23|2. **Source-fidelity check (pre-write gate):** confirm every Mandatory Rule maps to specific analysis evidence. If `EVIDENCE_INDEX[filename] == insufficient`, take the `[TO BE COMPLETED]` skeleton path.\n    24|3. **Compose** the file: `when_to_read` frontmatter (rules below) + the mandatory-format body. Apply the `ROUTING:` `## Purpose` prefix for the three routing files.\n    25|4. **Write** to `OUTPUT_PACK/{filename}` with `Write` (full-file, single tool call). Never `Bash`/`sed`/`awk`/`python3 <<EOF`/`cat >` to compose a file — that violates §10.5. Full-file `Write` is already the §10.5.1-safe path for non-ASCII content.\n    26|5. **Verify** the file exists and is non-empty; update `_progress.json` (`completed += 1`, append to `items[]`).\n    27|6. **Flush** the composed content from working memory before the next file.\n    28|\n    29|Tool discipline and the non-ASCII fallback are owned by `execution-protocol.md §10.5`/`§10.5.1` — do not restate them; cite them.\n    30|\n    31|## Mandatory file format (frontmatter-first)\n    32|\n    33|```markdown\n    34|---\n    35|when_to_read: |\n    36|  <triggers — see the authoring rules below>\n    37|---\n    38|\n    39|# <Title>\n    40|\n    41|## Purpose\n    42|<What decision or behavior this file governs. Prefix with `ROUTING:` for the three routing files only.>\n    43|\n    44|## Scope\n    45|<Teams, repositories, layers, or project areas this applies to>\n    46|\n    47|## Mandatory Rules\n    48|- <Specific, actionable rule derived from analysis.md>\n    49|\n    50|## Recommended Rules\n    51|- <Recommended practice; `[INFERRED]` allowed here only, tagged>\n    52|\n    53|## Restrictions and Prohibitions\n    54|- <What is explicitly prohibited>\n    55|\n    56|## Examples\n    57|- Valid: <example>\n    58|- Invalid: <example>\n    59|\n    60|## Exceptions\n    61|- <When deviation is allowed and who approves>\n    62|\n    63|## Traceability\n    64|- <analysis.md sections / source documents each rule derives from>\n    65|\n    66|## Version and Owner\n    67|- Version: 1.0\n    68|- Owner: <derived from source material or \"Project Team\">\n    69|```\n    70|\n    71|## The `when_to_read` frontmatter contract\n    72|\n    73|Every generated file MUST begin with YAML frontmatter carrying a `when_to_read` key. The\n    74|Stepwise loader parses this key from the leading `--- ... ---` block and renders it as a\n    75|`Read when:` line in the CONTEXT-PACK manifest the coding executor sees — so the agent\n    76|learns *when* to open the file **before** it decides to read it. A file with no\n    77|`when_to_read` still loads, but forfeits that routing signal.\n    78|\n    79|Authoring rules:\n    80|\n    81|1. **Derive triggers from what the file governs**, using the per-file `when_to_read\n    82|   triggers:` line in `references/file-catalog.md`. Do not invent triggers, and do not copy\n    83|   the file's `## Purpose` verbatim — Purpose says *what the file is*; `when_to_read` says\n    84|   *when to open it*.\n    85|2. **Be specific and imperative** — name the concrete activities. \"Read before writing or\n    86|   modifying any source file, choosing names, or structuring modules/imports.\" — not a\n    87|   topic label like \"Coding conventions.\"\n    88|3. **Use a YAML `|` block scalar.** The loader collapses interior newlines/whitespace into a\n    89|   single manifest line, so 1–3 lines is ideal; do not write a paragraph.\n    90|4. **Routing files** (`context-pack-index.md`, `project-inventory.md`, `codebase-map.md`):\n    91|   in addition to `when_to_read`, make the body's `## Purpose` **first line start with\n    92|   `ROUTING:`**. That prefix makes the loader tag the file `→ ROUTING (read first)` in the\n    93|   manifest, forcing the executor's mandatory pre-flight read. Non-routing files use a plain\n    94|   `## Purpose`.\n    95|5. **Emit `when_to_read` even for `[TO BE COMPLETED]` files.** The routing hint is valid\n    96|   regardless of body completeness — a thin file still tells the agent when it would matter.\n    97|\n    98|### Worked example — normal file (`coding-standards.md`)\n    99|\n   100|```markdown\n   101|---\n   102|when_to_read: |\n   103|  Read before writing or modifying any source file — choosing names, structuring\n   104|  modules/imports, handling errors, or adding logging.\n   105|---\n   106|\n   107|# Coding Standards\n   108|\n   109|## Purpose\n   110|Defines naming, code organization, formatting, error-handling, and logging rules that all\n   111|source code in this project must follow.\n   112|\n   113|## Scope\n   114|All application source across every service and package in the repository.\n   115|...\n   116|```\n   117|\n   118|### Worked example — routing file (`project-inventory.md`)\n   119|\n   120|```markdown\n   121|---\n   122|when_to_read: |\n   123|  Read FIRST, before locating code or planning any change — to resolve which project,\n   124|  namespace, key type, or command applies without crawling the tree.\n   125|---\n   126|\n   127|# Project Inventory\n   128|\n   129|## Purpose\n   130|ROUTING: Dense lookup index mapping projects, bounded contexts, key types, and verified\n   131|commands to their locations — the read-first entry point for finding where code lives.\n   132|\n   133|## Scope\n   134|All repositories, solutions, and packages in the engagement.\n   135|...\n   136|```\n   137|\n   138|### Worked example — insufficient-evidence file (`[TO BE COMPLETED]`)\n   139|\n   140|```markdown\n   141|---\n   142|when_to_read: |\n   143|  Read before configuring test data — choosing sources, applying anonymization, or setting\n   144|  reset/cleanup policy.\n   145|---\n   146|\n   147|# Test Data Policy\n   148|\n   149|## Purpose\n   150|Governs test-data sourcing, anonymization, and cleanup.\n   151|\n   152|status: pending — insufficient evidence in analysis.md\n   153|\n   154|## Mandatory Rules\n   155|- [TO BE COMPLETED] No test-data policy evidence found in the inputs. Provide test-data\n   156|  sources and privacy constraints, then re-run in REPAIR mode.\n   157|...\n   158|```\n   159|\n   160|## Zero Invention application (this phase)\n   161|\n   162|- Mandatory Rules come only from the file's `SOURCE_MAP` sections. No source → no rule →\n   163|  `[TO BE COMPLETED]`, never invented filler.\n   164|- `[INFERRED]` hints are allowed only in `## Recommended Rules`, tagged, and never promoted\n   165|  to Mandatory Rules.\n   166|- `## Traceability` must cite the analysis sections (and source documents) each rule came\n   167|  from — the audit trail that makes the pack verifiable.\n   168|\n</file>","isError":false}}
