{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01KN137MbBKLigRAHLKa6y92","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/.coda/skills/generate-context-pack/references/file-catalog.md\" lines=\"212\" start=\"1\" end=\"212\" returned=\"212\" has_more=\"false\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Context-Pack File Catalog\n     2|\n     3|## Context Contract\n     4|\n     5|- **H1 Title:** Context-Pack File Catalog\n     6|- **Inputs:** none directly — this is the reference catalog Phase A/B consult.\n     7|- **Outputs:** none — it defines, per file, what it governs, its `analysis.md` source mapping, and its `when_to_read` triggers.\n     8|- **Carries Forward:** the `SOURCE_MAP` and the per-file trigger text used by Phase B.\n     9|- **Flush After:** n/a (reference).\n    10|- **Dependency:** consulted by Phase A (Step A2/A3) and Phase B (compose step).\n    11|- **Used by step:** SKILL.md Step 2 and Step 3.\n    12|\n    13|Generate **only** the files below (plus `context-pack-index.md`, written last). Each entry\n    14|defines what the file governs, what to extract from the analysis, its `Source:` mapping, and\n    15|the `when_to_read triggers:` to author into the file's frontmatter (see\n    16|`phase-b-file-generation.md` for the frontmatter contract). The three **ROUTING** files get a\n    17|`## Purpose` that begins with `ROUTING:`.\n    18|\n    19|---\n    20|\n    21|**`domain.md`** — Domain language, business entities, and invariants\n    22|\n    23|- `## Mandatory Rules`: domain terminology that must be used consistently; entity names and their definitions; business invariants that must never be violated\n    24|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred ways to name domain concepts in code; bounded context boundaries\n    25|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: terms that must not be used (e.g., internal jargon vs. domain language); entity relationships that must not be violated\n    26|- Source: `## For domain.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n    27|- when_to_read triggers: Read before modeling entities, naming domain concepts in code, or writing business logic — to use the ubiquitous language and respect invariants.\n    28|\n    29|---\n    30|\n    31|**`arch-standards.md`** — Architecture style, layering, integration patterns\n    32|\n    33|- `## Mandatory Rules`: approved architecture style (e.g., hexagonal, layered, microservices); layer boundaries and what can depend on what; integration patterns (sync vs. async, REST vs. events); observability requirements\n    34|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred patterns for new services; resilience patterns\n    35|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: architectural anti-patterns to avoid; prohibited cross-layer dependencies\n    36|- Source: `## For arch-standards.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n    37|- when_to_read triggers: Read before adding a component/service, wiring an integration, or placing code across layers — to respect layer boundaries and approved integration patterns.\n    38|\n    39|---\n    40|\n    41|**`tech-policy.md`** — Approved technology stack, tools, and versions\n    42|\n    43|- `## Mandatory Rules`: approved languages and runtime versions; approved frameworks and their versions; approved databases, messaging systems, cloud services; licensing requirements\n    44|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred libraries for common needs; preferred tooling\n    45|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited frameworks or libraries; deprecated dependencies to remove; unlicensed or unsupported tools\n    46|- Source: `## For tech-policy.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n    47|- when_to_read triggers: Read before adding a dependency, choosing a framework/library, or picking a runtime/version — to stay within the approved stack and licensing.\n    48|\n    49|---\n    50|\n    51|**`coding-standards.md`** — Naming conventions, code organization, style rules\n    52|\n    53|- `## Mandatory Rules`: naming conventions for files, classes, functions, variables; code organization rules (module structure, import order); linting and formatting rules (extracted from config files); error handling patterns; logging rules\n    54|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred patterns for common coding tasks; documentation conventions\n    55|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited patterns (e.g., `any` in TypeScript, raw SQL, magic numbers); style violations\n    56|- Source: `## For coding-standards.md` sections in `analysis.md`\n    57|- when_to_read triggers: Read before writing or modifying any source file — choosing names, structuring modules/imports, handling errors, or adding logging.\n    58|\n    59|---\n    60|\n    61|**`constraints.md`** — Hard limits and non-negotiable project constraints\n    62|\n    63|- `## Mandatory Rules`: hard limits on compliance, hosting, geography, performance, integrations; non-negotiable organizational constraints; known dependencies that cannot change\n    64|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred approaches within constraints\n    65|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: what is explicitly out of scope or prohibited by constraint\n    66|- Source: `## For constraints.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n    67|- when_to_read triggers: Read before making any design or scoping decision — to check hard compliance, hosting, geography, and performance limits before committing.\n    68|\n    69|---\n    70|\n    71|**`security.md`** — Authentication, authorization, secret management, encryption\n    72|\n    73|- `## Mandatory Rules`: authentication and authorization requirements; secret management rules (no hardcoded secrets, required vault/env patterns); encryption requirements (at rest, in transit); input validation requirements; dependency vulnerability rules\n    74|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred security patterns; security review expectations\n    75|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited patterns (hardcoded credentials, unencrypted sensitive data, unauthenticated endpoints)\n    76|- Source: `## For security.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n    77|- when_to_read triggers: Read before implementing auth, handling secrets/tokens, validating input, exposing an endpoint, or touching encryption.\n    78|\n    79|---\n    80|\n    81|**`customer-background.md`** — Customer context, business goals, stakeholder expectations\n    82|\n    83|- `## Mandatory Rules`: key business goals that must be respected; stakeholder expectations that constrain decisions; client-side constraints\n    84|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred ways to align with customer priorities\n    85|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: decisions that would conflict with customer expectations\n    86|- Source: `## For customer-background.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n    87|- when_to_read triggers: Read before prioritizing work or making a product trade-off — to align with the customer's business goals and stakeholder expectations.\n    88|\n    89|---\n    90|\n    91|**`test-standards.md`** — Test case format, coverage expectations, quality gates\n    92|\n    93|- `## Mandatory Rules`: required test types (unit, integration, E2E); coverage thresholds; test case format and traceability requirements; severity and priority classification rules; quality gates before release\n    94|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred testing patterns; test naming conventions\n    95|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited testing shortcuts; what must not be skipped\n    96|- Source: `## For test-standards.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n    97|- when_to_read triggers: Read before writing or reviewing tests, setting coverage gates, or classifying test severity/priority.\n    98|\n    99|---\n   100|\n   101|**`automation-standards.md`** — Test automation framework, patterns, CI execution\n   102|\n   103|- `## Mandatory Rules`: approved automation framework and version; page object or fixture patterns; locator strategy (preferred selectors); wait and retry rules; CI execution requirements\n   104|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred patterns for test organization; flaky test handling\n   105|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited locator strategies; prohibited test patterns\n   106|- Source: `## For automation-standards.md` sections in `analysis.md`\n   107|- when_to_read triggers: Read before authoring automated tests or CI test steps — to use the approved framework, locator strategy, and wait/retry rules.\n   108|\n   109|---\n   110|\n   111|**`test-data-policy.md`** — Test data sources, anonymization, cleanup\n   112|\n   113|- `## Mandatory Rules`: allowed test data sources; anonymization requirements; data reset and cleanup policy; privacy and compliance restrictions on test data\n   114|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred test data management patterns\n   115|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited data sources; data that must never appear in tests\n   116|- Source: `## For env-config.md and config-management-rules.md` and any data policy sections in `analysis.md`\n   117|- when_to_read triggers: Read before creating or loading test data — choosing sources, applying anonymization, or setting reset/cleanup policy.\n   118|\n   119|---\n   120|\n   121|**`env-config.md`** — Test environments, browsers, accounts, feature flags\n   122|\n   123|- `## Mandatory Rules`: required test environments and their URLs; browser and device matrix; required test accounts and access prerequisites; feature flag configuration for testing\n   124|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred environment setup patterns\n   125|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: environments that must not be used for testing; prohibited configuration patterns\n   126|- Source: `## For env-config.md and config-management-rules.md` sections in `analysis.md`\n   127|- when_to_read triggers: Read before running against an environment or setting up test accounts — to pick the right env/URL, browser matrix, and feature-flag config.\n   128|\n   129|---\n   130|\n   131|**`best-practices.md`** — General best practices not covered by other files\n   132|\n   133|- `## Mandatory Rules`: cross-cutting practices that apply everywhere; team conventions that must be followed\n   134|- `## Recommended Rules`: general quality expectations; preferred approaches for common situations\n   135|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: anti-patterns to avoid across the project\n   136|- Source: `## For best-practices.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n   137|- when_to_read triggers: Read when a cross-cutting question arises not covered by a more specific file — to follow team conventions and avoid known anti-patterns.\n   138|\n   139|---\n   140|\n   141|**`config-management-rules.md`** — Environment configuration, secret injection, config file conventions\n   142|\n   143|- `## Mandatory Rules`: how environment variables must be named and injected; config file conventions; secret injection patterns; environment-specific configuration rules\n   144|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred config management patterns\n   145|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited config patterns (hardcoded values, committed secrets, environment-specific code)\n   146|- Source: `## For env-config.md and config-management-rules.md` sections in `analysis.md`\n   147|- when_to_read triggers: Read before adding config or environment variables, injecting secrets, or writing environment-specific settings.\n   148|\n   149|---\n   150|\n   151|**`infrastructure.md`** — Infrastructure topology, deployment targets, cloud/on-prem resources\n   152|\n   153|- `## Mandatory Rules`: approved cloud providers, regions, and services; required infrastructure-as-code tooling (Terraform, Pulumi, etc.); container orchestration requirements (Kubernetes, ECS, etc.); networking and VPC rules; required tagging and naming conventions for resources\n   154|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred deployment patterns; preferred IaC module structure; recommended observability stack (metrics, logs, traces)\n   155|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: prohibited cloud services or regions; manual infrastructure changes outside IaC; unapproved container base images\n   156|- Source: `## For infrastructure.md` sections in `analysis.md` and `meeting-analysis.md`\n   157|- when_to_read triggers: Read before provisioning resources, writing IaC, configuring deployment/networking, or choosing a cloud service/region.\n   158|\n   159|---\n   160|\n   161|**`validation-tools.md`** — Project-specific tool registry for build, test, lint, typecheck, and security checks\n   162|\n   163|- `## Mandatory Rules`: exact commands to run for build, test, lint, typecheck, and security scanning; required tool versions; order of execution for pre-commit and CI checks; commands that must pass before any code is merged\n   164|- `## Recommended Rules`: preferred flags and options for each tool; how to run subsets of tests; how to fix common tool failures\n   165|- `## Restrictions and Prohibitions`: tools or commands that must not be used; deprecated scripts to avoid; commands that must not be run in production environments\n   166|- Source: `## For validation-tools.md` sections in `analysis.md`; also extracted from `Makefile`, `package.json` scripts, `pyproject.toml`, CI pipeline definitions\n   167|- when_to_read triggers: Read before running build/test/lint/typecheck/security checks or wiring pre-commit/CI — to use the exact project commands and required pass order.\n   168|\n   169|---\n   170|\n   171|**`project-inventory.md`** — Structured lookup index for projects, bounded contexts, key types, and verified commands **(ROUTING)**\n   172|\n   173|- `## Purpose`: (ROUTING) explain that this file is the dense lookup table for locating the right project, namespace, class, or file without craw