{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01GzMXeceG1Q6wshqYVzQn5r","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/_dev/GLB/stepwise-fifa-qa/context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"739\" start=\"116\" end=\"415\" returned=\"300\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   116|## Section files\n   117|\n   118|Non-universal sections now live as individual files under `execution-protocol/`.\n   119|Read only those your route selects (see the routing rule above); each is readable\n   120|standalone with its original § numbering intact. The **universal** sections (§2,\n   121|§10.5, §11 — required by every skill) remain inlined below.\n   122|\n   123|| § | Location |\n   124||---|----------|\n   125|| §1  Session Identity | `execution-protocol/01-session-identity.md` |\n   126|| §2  `_progress.json` | inline below (universal) |\n   127|| §3  IMPL-STATE | `execution-protocol/03-impl-state.md` |\n   128|| §4  Memory Bank | `execution-protocol/04-memory-bank.md` |\n   129|| §5  FIC recovery | `execution-protocol/05-fic-recovery.md` |\n   130|| §6  Dual-format | `execution-protocol/06-dual-format.md` |\n   131|| §7  REPAIR / §7.3 REBUILD | `execution-protocol/07-repair-mode.md` |\n   132|| §8  Deviations | `execution-protocol/08-deviations.md` |\n   133|| §9  Non-interactive | `execution-protocol/09-non-interactive.md` |\n   134|| §10 Section generation | `execution-protocol/10-section-generation.md` |\n   135|| §10.5 Tool discipline | inline below (universal) |\n   136|| §11 Output sidecar | inline below (universal) |\n   137|| §12 Delegated exploration | `execution-protocol/12-delegated-exploration.md` |\n   138|| §13 Code-location | `execution-protocol/13-code-location.md` |\n   139|| §14 Parallel fan-out | `execution-protocol/14-parallel-fanout.md` |\n   140|| §15 Deliverable upload safety | `execution-protocol/15-deliverable-upload-safety.md` |\n   141|\n   142|A skill's route (e.g. `§2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11`) resolves each §N via this table:\n   143|universal sections are already here; the rest are one file-read away. Existing\n   144|\"see execution-protocol.md §N\" citations in the 123 skills keep working through\n   145|this one redirect hop — no skill edits required.\n   146|\n   147|---\n   148|\n   149|## 2. _progress.json — Early Signal Write\n   150|\n   151|**Purpose:** Prevents the orchestrator coordinator from sending SIGINT to a running skill.\n   152|\n   153|Write this as the **FIRST file action** in any skill execution (before creating any other outputs):\n   154|\n   155|```json\n   156|{\n   157|  \"skill\": \"{skill-name}\",\n   158|  \"session_id\": \"{SESSION_ID}\",\n   159|  \"status\": \"RUNNING\",\n   160|  \"started_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC, e.g. 2026-08-15T13:21:37Z}\",\n   161|  \"completed_at\": null,\n   162|  \"total\": 0,\n   163|  \"completed\": 0,\n   164|  \"items\": []\n   165|}\n   166|```\n   167|\n   168|Write to: `{output_folder}/_progress.json`\n   169|\n   170|**Update cadence:**\n   171|- On FIC trigger (Section 5): `\"status\": \"COMPACTION_NEEDED\"`\n   172|- On completion: `\"status\": \"COMPLETED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   173|- On failure: `\"status\": \"FAILED\"`, `\"completed_at\": \"{ISO-8601 UTC}\"`\n   174|\n   175|**UTC, not local time with a `Z` on it.** Read the real clock and convert. A local timestamp\n   176|labelled `Z` reads as a lie about when the work ran — Stepwise stamps its own state in true UTC,\n   177|so the two disagree by your offset and every reviewer sees fabricated evidence where there was\n   178|only a wrong clock. If you cannot get UTC, omit the field rather than guess.\n   179|\n   180|**Status vocabulary — closed set.** Every value carries distinct semantics; the set is\n   181|closed so a monitor can classify any tracker without knowing which skill wrote it.\n   182|\n   183|| status | meaning | live? |\n   184||---|---|---|\n   185|| `RUNNING` | step is executing | yes |\n   186|| `PARTIAL` | chunked step finished its chunk with work remaining; a later invocation resumes | yes |\n   187|| `COMPACTION_NEEDED` | FIC trigger (§5); awaiting compaction | yes |\n   188|| `COMPLETED` | all declared work done | no — terminal |\n   189|| `FAILED` | step could not complete | no — terminal |\n   190|| `SCOPE_REFUSED` | input exceeded the skill's scope envelope; step declined and recommended decomposition rather than producing a degraded artifact | no — terminal |\n   191|\n   192|**Never invent a synonym for a value already in the table** — `IN_PROGRESS`, `STARTED`,\n   193|`OK`, `DONE` and similar are prohibited precisely because they mean `RUNNING` or\n   194|`COMPLETED` while reading as unrecognized to a monitor keying on the set above.\n   195|\n   196|Adding a genuinely new state (distinct semantics, not a synonym) means adding a row to\n   197|this table in the same change. Do not introduce one ad hoc in a single skill: an\n   198|undocumented status is indistinguishable from a typo at read time.\n   199|\n   200|Item-level statuses inside `items[]` / `sections{}` are a **separate** vocabulary and are\n   201|not governed by this table — `\"status\": \"complete\"` on an entry is fine.\n   202|\n   203|### 2.a Seed the denominator (list shape — MANDATORY)\n   204|\n   205|The skeleton in the template above ships `\"total\": 0` because the count is not yet known\n   206|at FIRST ACTION. It is a placeholder, **not** the value you leave behind.\n   207|\n   208|**As soon as the item count is known — and before the first item is produced — write\n   209|`total` with the real number.** For most skills this is the end of the planning/discovery\n   210|step, once the catalog of services / contexts / ADRs / units / diagrams exists:\n   211|\n   212|```\n   213|total = len(ITEMS_TO_PRODUCE)      # + any fixed tail artifacts (manifest, audit)\n   214|UPDATE _progress.json → { \"total\": total }\n   215|```\n   216|\n   217|A tracker still reporting `total: 0` after production has begun is a defect, not a\n   218|cosmetic gap: `0/0` is indistinguishable from \"hung before doing anything\", which is\n   219|exactly the ambiguity this file exists to remove. Observed 2026-08-09\n   220|(`establishing-architecture-foundation`): a 27-minute run showed `0/0` end to end while\n   221|12 service files and a 36 KB manifest were being written.\n   222|\n   223|### 2.b Co-locate the tracker update with the checkpoint write (MANDATORY)\n   224|\n   225|Skills with a Write-Flush-Forget production loop (§5) typically maintain **two** state\n   226|files: `_checkpoint.json` (rich resume state, internal) and `_progress.json` (thin\n   227|liveness signal, read by the orchestrator and by humans). The observed failure is that\n   228|per-item bookkeeping lands in the checkpoint and never in the tracker — the loop is\n   229|invisible from outside for its entire duration.\n   230|\n   231|**Rule: the `_progress.json` update is part of the same loop step as the checkpoint\n   232|write. Never one without the other.** Both go in the same numbered sub-step so a skipped\n   233|write is visible when reading the skill:\n   234|\n   235|```\n   236|## WRITE CHECKPOINT + UPDATE PROGRESS (single step — never split)\n   237|   1. Serialize INDEX to CHECKPOINT_FILE (_checkpoint.json)\n   238|   2. UPDATE _progress.json → completed += 1, items += [{id, status: \"complete\", file}]\n   239|```\n   240|\n   241|This applies to every discrete unit the loop emits: per-service, per-context, per-ADR,\n   242|per-unit, per-diagram, per-tab.\n   243|\n   244|**Timestamps come from a real clock read.** `started_at` and `completed_at` MUST be\n   245|actual timestamps at the moment of writing — never round numbers back-filled at the end\n   246|to describe what the run \"would have\" done. A `completed_at` that postdates the file's\n   247|own mtime is a fabricated tracker and fails §2.1 check 5.\n   248|\n   249|**Schema variants.** A skill picks ONE of the three shapes below based on its work model\n   250|and uses it consistently for the whole run. The common envelope (`skill`, `session_id`,\n   251|`status`, `started_at`, `completed_at`) is identical across variants.\n   252|\n   253|- **List shape (default — shown above)** — uses `total`, `completed`, `items[]`. Suits\n   254|  skills that produce many discrete artifacts (e.g. `implementing-code` writing source\n   255|  files; `planning-code-tasks` tracking task entries).\n   256|- **Section shape** — uses `skeleton_written` (bool) + `sections{}` map. Suits skills\n   257|  that produce a single multi-section spec file from large unstructured inputs. See\n   258|  **Section 10** for the full protocol (skeleton-first + one-section-per-write +\n   259|  source extraction + CONTINUE re-entry).\n   260|- **Hybrid shape** — carries BOTH: `skeleton_written` + `sections{}` for the manifest,\n   261|  AND `total` / `completed` / `items[]` for the per-item files produced alongside it.\n   262|  Suits skills that emit a sectioned manifest *and* a fan-out of per-entry artifacts\n   263|  (e.g. `secreview-static-scanning`: `sections{}` tracks the SCAN-MANIFEST while\n   264|  `items[]` grows one entry per finding). See §10.1.\n   265|\n   266|**A hybrid tracker MUST say so.** State the variant in the FIRST ACTION callout —\n   267|\"write `_progress.json` using the **hybrid** variant (per §10.1)\" — so a reader can tell\n   268|a deliberate hybrid from a skill that drifted between two shapes. Carrying both field\n   269|groups *without* that declaration is the drift failure §2.1 check 5 catches:\n   270|`establishing-architecture-foundation` (fixed 2026-08-09) incremented list-shape counters\n   271|in one phase and wrote `sections{}` in the next, leaving no coherent denominator.\n   272|\n   273|Both halves of a hybrid follow their own rules: §2.a seeds `total` for the `items[]` half,\n   274|§10 governs the `sections{}` half. `secreview-memory-synthesis` and the `pentest-*` family\n   275|are the reference implementations.\n   276|\n   277|### Skill-Level Enforcement (FIRST ACTION / LAST ACTION)\n   278|\n   279|Every skill MUST include these two inline callouts — agents do not reliably follow\n   280|protocol-only references for session bookend writes:\n   281|\n   282|**At session start** (after folder creation, before any other file write):\n   283|\n   284|> **FIRST ACTION — MANDATORY:** Write `_progress.json` to the output folder before any other file write.\n   285|> This prevents the orchestrator from sending SIGINT.\n   286|\n   287|Include the minimal JSON schema inline in the skill so the agent can write immediately\n   288|without reading this file first. Use `\"session_id\": \"initializing\"` as placeholder.\n   289|\n   290|**At session end** (after all spec files written, after Memory Bank writes):\n   291|\n   292|> **LAST ACTION — MANDATORY:** Update `_progress.json` status to `COMPLETED` with `completed_at` timestamp.\n   293|> If the session failed, set status to `FAILED` instead.\n   294|\n   295|These callouts are NOT infrastructure duplication — they are the minimum viable bridge\n   296|between protocol and agent behavior. The full lifecycle lives here; the callouts trigger it.\n   297|\n   298|**For `implementing-code` only** — also include `output_contract` (survives compaction):\n   299|\n   300|```json\n   301|{\n   302|  \"skill\": \"implementing-code\",\n   303|  \"session_id\": \"{SESSION_ID}\",\n   304|  \"status\": \"RUNNING\",\n   305|  \"output_contract\": {\n   306|    \"progress_folder_path\": \"{progress_folder_path}\",\n   307|    \"impl_state_file\": \"IMPL-STATE-{session_id}.md\",\n   308|    \"source_path\": \"{source_path}\",\n   309|    \"expected_outputs\": [\"source_path\", \"impl_state_path\"]\n   310|  }\n   311|}\n   312|```\n   313|\n   314|Pin the same values in the IMPL-STATE header under `output_contract`. After context\n   315|compaction, read IMPL-STATE header (not filesystem) to recover the output path and format.\n   316|\n   317|---\n   318|\n   319|### 2.1 Pre-Completion Self-Validation Gate (universal — generative skills)\n   320|\n   321|**Purpose:** Prevent predictable, repeatable gaps from reaching the human quality gate.\n   322|A generative step MUST self-validate against the checklist below **immediately before**\n   323|the LAST ACTION completion write (`_progress.json.status: COMPLETED`). This runs on the\n   324|agent's own output, before any human sees it.\n   325|\n   326|Run all five checks. Each is tech-agnostic — no framework, language, or project name.\n   327|\n   328|1. **Input-contract completeness.** Every required input, section, and output-contract\n   329|   item declared by the skill/step is addressed in the artifact. No declared section is\n   330|   empty, stubbed, or silently omitted.\n   331|2. **No unresolved placeholders.** The artifact contains no lingering `[TBD]`,\n   332|   `[to be identified]`, `[PLACEHOLDER]`, `TODO`, or unanswered `OQ-###` / open-question\n   333|   left as if resolved. Each is either resolved from available evidence OR surfaced\n   334|   explicitly as a pending item / assumption / blocker — never left silent.\n   335|3. **Context-pack / source grounding.** Every non-trivial claim, path, identifier,\n   336|   technology, decision, estimate, and status traces to context-pack, upstream artifacts,\n   337|   or the source under analysis. Nothing is invented. If evidence is missing, mark the\n   338|   uncertainty visible rather than fabricating a resolution.\n   339|4. **Format-contract conformance.** Output matches the skill's declared structure:\n   340|   headings, stable IDs, tables, schemas, traceability links, filename, and status\n   341|   conventions.\n   342|5. **Tracker integrity.** `_progress.json` is a truthful record of the run, not a summary\n   343|   written at the end. Check the half/halves your declared §2 variant actually uses:\n   344|   - **list half** (list or hybrid): `total > 0` — a `COMPLETED` step still showing\n   345|     `total: 0` never seeded its denominator per §2.a — and `completed == total`.\n   346|   - **section half** (section or hybrid): `skeleton_written == true` and every declared\n   347|     section has a terminal status.\n   348|   - **all variants**: the tracker matches the variant the skill declared at FIRST ACTION\n   349|     and did not drift into another mid-run; `status` is from the §2 closed set; and\n   350|     `started_at` / `completed_at` are real clock reads, with `completed_at` not\n   351|     postdating the write itself.\n   352|\n   353|   Carrying both field groups is NOT a failure when the skill declared the hybrid variant\n   354|   — that is the sanctioned third shape. It IS a failure when the skill declared list or\n   355|   section and grew the other half mid-run.\n   356|\n   357|**On failure of any check:**\n   358|- If self-repairable from available evidence → repair, then re-run the checklist.\n   359|- If it requires information the agent does not have → do **not** mark `COMPLETED`.\n   360|  Record the gap as a visible pending item / open question / assumption in the artifact\n   361|  and (when a blocker) escalate per §8 Deviation taxonomy. Marking a step COMPLETED with\n   362|  a known, silent gap is a protocol violation.\n   363|\n   364|**Skill-Level Enforcement.** Generative skills SHOULD include a one-line inline callout\n   365|at their finalization step so the agent triggers this without reading the protocol first:\n   366|\n   367|> **PRE-COMPLETION CHECK — MANDATORY:** Before the LAST ACTION completion write, self-validate\n   368|> the artifact against §2.1 (input-contract completeness · no unresolved placeholders ·\n   369|> context-pack grounding · format conformance · tracker integrity). Repair or surface gaps;\n   370|> never complete silently. A `COMPLETED` tracker still showing `total: 0` fails check 5.\n   371|\n   372|This gate is the counterpart to the FIRST/LAST ACTION callouts in §2: FIRST ACTION opens\n   373|the step, §2.1 qualifies the output, LAST ACTION closes it.\n   374|\n   375|---\n   376|\n   377|\n   378|---\n   379|\n   380|### 10.5 Tool Discipline (Hard Rules)\n   381|\n   382|These rules exist because each one has been observed as a failure mode. Violations defeat the protocol even when the prose discipline appears to be followed.\n   383|\n   384|- **Skeleton write: use `Write`.** Single tool call, single file creation. Do NOT compose the skeleton via `Bash` heredoc.\n   385|- **Section population: use `Edit`.** Targeted find-and-replace, one section per call. The `Edit` tool handles unicode natively and validates the prior content matches what you expect.\n   386|- **NEVER use `Bash` + `sed` / `awk` / `python3 <<EOF` / `cat >` to mutate SPEC_FILE.** These are bulk-rewrite paths disguised as targeted operations. They read the whole file into memory, transform it, and write it back — the exact pattern Phase B is designed to prevent. They also bypass `Edit`'s prior-content validation, so a bug in the script silently corrupts the spec.\n   387|- **If `Edit` fails on a section, fix the `Edit` call.** Provide more surrounding context to make `old_string` unique. Do NOT switch to `Bash` / `python3` as a workaround — that path has been observed to cascade: agent hits one snag, then rewrites all remaining sections in a single script, violating one-section-per-write.\n   388|- **NEVER compose multiple sections in memory before writing.**\n   389|- **NEVER emit more than one section per Write/Edit call.**\n   390|- **NEVER re-read raw sources during Phase B.** Read `_extractions/*.md` files, or SPEC_FILE for declared cross-section state.\n   391|- **Prefer ASCII punctuation in authored content.** Emit ASCII equivalents for *decorative* non-ASCII glyphs the model tends to insert: `-` or ` - ` for em/en dashes (`—`/`–`), `->` for `→`, straight quotes `\"` / `'` for smart quotes (`“ ” ‘ ’`), `...` for `…`, `-`/`*` for `•`, `x` for `×`, plain space for non-breaking space. This keeps English-output specs on the fast, safe targeted-`Edit` path by avoiding the §10.5.1 non-ASCII fallback (which forces full-file replace). **This rule removes gratuitous glyphs only — it NEVER changes the output language or strips *required* non-ASCII.** Accented and localized characters in non-English output (`á é í ó ú ñ ü ç ã …`) are content, not decoration: preserve them exactly and let §10.5.1 handle the run. Do not transliterate them to \"fix\" the fallback.\n   392|\n   393|If a section's content genuinely will not fit a single `Edit` call (extreme size), split that one section into sub-edits — but every sub-edit is still an `Edit` call against SPEC_FILE, never a Bash mutation.\n   394|\n   395|---\n   396|\n   397|#### 10.5.1 Non-ASCII Content Fallback (TEMPORARY — runtime workaround)\n   398|\n   399|**Status:** TEMPORARY. Remove this fallback once the runtime's targeted-edit operation supports UTF-8 reliably.\n   400|\n   401|**Why this exists.** Some runtimes' targeted-edit operations have been observed to fail with `'ascii' codec can't encode character ...` errors when the `new_string` contains non-ASCII codepoints (accented vowels in Spanish / French / Portuguese / German, em dashes, smart quotes, etc.). The same failure mode has been observed to **destroy the target file** (the tool truncates the file before catching the encode exception, so a failed edit zeroes out the prior content). Until the runtime is fixed, targeted-edit cannot be trusted for SPEC_FILEs that may contain non-ASCII text.\n   402|\n   403|**Detection (at Phase B entry).** If ANY of the following is true, apply this fallback for **every** section write in the run:\n   404|- The detected output language (per Step 2) is not English.\n   405|- Any input source or `_extractions/*.md` file contains a character with codepoint > 127.\n   406|- Detection is unreliable / ambiguous — default to this fallback. The cost is bounded.\n   407|\n   408|**Per-section flow under fallback:**\n   409|\n   410|1. **Read** SPEC_FILE from disk — this is the current state (skeleton + sections populated so far).\n   411|2. **Generate** this section's body in working memory using the inputs from Phase B step 1.\n   412|3. **Compose** the full file payload in working memory:\n   413|   - header / metadata block (verbatim from disk)\n   414|   - all previously populated sections (verbatim from disk)\n   415|   - this section, freshly populated\n</file>","isError":false}}
