{"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"toolu_01HWhCpLwstS74SPaKmAM3k6","result":"<file path=\"/Users/andres.mesa/_dev/GLB/stepwise-fifa-qa/context-pack/system/execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"739\" start=\"514\" end=\"738\" returned=\"225\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n   514|## 11. Harness Output Sidecar\n   515|\n   516|**Purpose.** The Stepwise harness reads each step's declared outputs from a sidecar JSON file written at a path the harness controls. If this file is missing, the orchestrator records `outputs: {}` in `state.json`, the UI's artifact panel renders empty, and downstream steps cannot pick up output values via propagation. **Artifacts on disk are not enough** — the sidecar is the framework contract between the skill and the orchestrator.\n   517|\n   518|**Applicability.** Any skill that may be invoked by Stepwise. Standalone invocations (no orchestrator) do not need the sidecar — see the trigger gate below.\n   519|\n   520|**When this section applies.** The input prompt contains a `## Run metadata` block with an `output_file = '<absolute path>'` line. If that block is absent (e.g. standalone invocation outside Stepwise), skip the sidecar write entirely.\n   521|\n   522|---\n   523|\n   524|### 11.1 Mechanism\n   525|\n   526|```\n   527|1. Re-read the prompt's `## Run metadata` block. Copy the path on the\n   528|   `output_file = '...'` line VERBATIM. Do NOT reconstruct it from runId,\n   529|   session name, project name, or any other source — the path the harness\n   530|   wrote there is the path the harness expects to read from.\n   531|\n   532|2. Re-read the prompt's `## Output parameters` table. For EVERY row in that\n   533|   table, include a key in the JSON object:\n   534|     - key   = the parameter name from the table\n   535|     - value = the resolved value the skill produced for that output\n   536|               (typically the path to the artifact folder or file)\n   537|\n   538|3. Write that JSON object to the `output_file` path. This is the FINAL file\n   539|   write of the run — strict ordering (see Section 11.4):\n   540|     - AFTER any skill-defined output verification (e.g. file-existence checks).\n   541|     - AFTER Memory Bank session-end writes (Section 4).\n   542|     - AFTER `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   543|\n   544|4. Do NOT emit any tool call after the sidecar write. The next event in the\n   545|   transcript should be the `final_response` summary.\n   546|```\n   547|\n   548|---\n   549|\n   550|### 11.1.1 Path Correctness Rule (mandatory)\n   551|\n   552|The value reported for any output **path** parameter MUST be the **actual on-disk path the skill wrote\n   553|to** — never a re-derived input parameter, never a fresh `value_template` substitution, and never a\n   554|scope-/sibling-adjusted path that was NOT the one actually used for the write.\n   555|\n   556|```\n   557|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every path-typed output parameter:\n   558|  1. Confirm the artifact exists at the path you are about to report\n   559|     (e.g. `ls <PATH>/<EXPECTED-FILE>*` or a stat/existence check).\n   560|  2. If the path the skill actually wrote to differs from the input parameter\n   561|     for ANY reason — sibling-folder rule, scope adjustment, feature_id stripping,\n   562|     SESSION_ID derivation — report the ACTUAL written path, NOT the parameter.\n   563|  3. Reporting a parameter you did not write to is the documented cause of the\n   564|     duplicate-artifact-on-REPAIR defect: a later REPAIR run is handed the wrong\n   565|     folder, finds no prior artifact, and silently rebuilds a duplicate. (See §7.)\n   566|```\n   567|\n   568|**Worked example (sibling rule).** A planning step receives `planning_output_path =\n   569|/base/code-development/code-task-planning` but, because `feature_id` was empty and the upstream research\n   570|was scoped, Step 1 actually wrote to `/base/code-development/TICKET-001/code-task-planning/`. The sidecar\n   571|MUST report the latter (the path that holds the files), never the flat input parameter.\n   572|\n   573|> **Note for skill authors:** \"report the parameter VERBATIM\" guidance (used by some skills to avoid\n   574|> pre-resolution double-prefixing) means *do not re-prepend project/scope segments yourself* — it does\n   575|> NOT license reporting a path you did not write to. When your own logic diverges from the parameter,\n   576|> the actual written path always wins.\n   577|\n   578|---\n   579|\n   580|### 11.1.2 Verdict Fidelity Rule (mandatory)\n   581|\n   582|§11.1.1 governs path-typed outputs. This rule governs **closed-set outputs** — any output parameter\n   583|that declares an enumerated `values:` array, which in practice means every gate / verdict-gated-transition\n   584|(VGT) verdict the orchestrator routes on.\n   585|\n   586|The value reported for such a parameter MUST be **one of the declared tokens, copied character-for-character**.\n   587|\n   588|```\n   589|BEFORE writing the sidecar, for every output parameter with a declared `values:` set:\n   590|  1. Re-read the allowed tokens from the prompt's `## Output parameters` table\n   591|     (or, when the table omits them, from this skill's own verdict definition).\n   592|  2. Emit one of those tokens EXACTLY: same spelling, same case, same underscores.\n   593|     No synonym, no title-casing, no pluralisation, no added qualifier, no\n   594|     translation, no \"PASSED (with notes)\".\n   595|  3. Confirm the emitted token is a member of the declared set — a literal string\n   596|     comparison, not \"close enough\". If your computed verdict does not map onto a\n   597|     declared token, that is a bug in your mapping: pick the correct declared token,\n   598|     do not invent one.\n   599|  4. Confirm the token AGREES with the artifact you just wrote. When the report /\n   600|     spec carries the verdict in its own front-matter or summary, the two MUST be\n   601|     identical. A report that says PASSED and a sidecar that says something else is\n   602|     a self-contradiction, and the sidecar is what the orchestrator acts on.\n   603|```\n   604|\n   605|**Why this is not cosmetic.** The orchestrator matches the reported token against the step's\n   606|`gate_policy` accept/reject lists. A token in neither list is **unroutable**, so the engine\n   607|**fails closed and treats it as a reject** — the only safe default, since silently passing an\n   608|unrecognised verdict through a correctness gate would be worse. The consequence is a spurious\n   609|retry loop: the upstream producer step is re-run, every step between it and the gate is reset and\n   610|re-run with it, and the retry budget is consumed. When the budget is exhausted the run escalates\n   611|to a human gate carrying a rejection that never happened.\n   612|\n   613|**Worked example (observed).** A plan-verification gate declared\n   614|`values: [PASSED, PASSED_WITH_FINDINGS, FAILED]`. The skill wrote a report whose front-matter read\n   615|`verification_verdict: PASSED`, `weighted_total: 100`, `failed_blocker_count: 0` — a clean pass — but\n   616|emitted `\"verification_verdict\": \"APPROVED\"` in the sidecar. `APPROVED` is not in the set, so the gate\n   617|failed closed, rejected, and re-ran planning plus the two steps between. A perfect-scoring plan was\n   618|re-planned because the sidecar used a synonym.\n   619|\n   620|**Robustness note.** This write is the last action of a run, often after a long generation, and may be\n   621|performed under a degraded executor — a fallback model, a smaller tier after a quota or rate-limit\n   622|failure, or a resumed session. Those are exactly the conditions under which a token gets paraphrased.\n   623|Treat the closed-set comparison in step 3 as a real check to perform at write time, not a stylistic\n   624|preference — it is cheap, and it is the only thing standing between a correct verdict and a wasted lap.\n   625|\n   626|---\n   627|\n   628|### 11.2 Example\n   629|\n   630|For a skill that declares a single output parameter named `foo_output_path`:\n   631|\n   632|```json\n   633|{ \"foo_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/<capability>/<step>\" }\n   634|```\n   635|\n   636|For a skill with multiple output parameters, include every row from the `## Output parameters` table — one key per row. The skill-specific parameter names live in each SKILL.md (Section 11.5); this section does not enumerate them.\n   637|\n   638|---\n   639|\n   640|### 11.3 Violation\n   641|\n   642|Ending a Stepwise run with a `final_response` summary that lists the generated artifacts but no sidecar write breaks output propagation. The harness MAY have a `value_template` fallback for specific outputs, but that fallback is not guaranteed for every skill / every output. Treat the sidecar write as **non-optional** whenever the `## Run metadata` block is present in the prompt — do not rely on the fallback.\n   643|\n   644|Skills whose outputs lack a `value_template` fallback fail **silently** when the sidecar is missing: downstream steps see `outputs: {}` and propagate empty values; no error surfaces in the run. This is the failure mode this section prevents.\n   645|\n   646|---\n   647|\n   648|### 11.4 Composition with Section 2 LAST ACTION\n   649|\n   650|The sidecar write extends Section 2's LAST ACTION; it does NOT replace it. Strict ordering for any Stepwise-invoked skill:\n   651|\n   652|1. All skill-specific outputs written and verified.\n   653|2. Memory Bank writes (Section 4 — `active-context.md`, `progress.md`).\n   654|3. `_progress.json` flipped to `status: COMPLETED` (Section 2 LAST ACTION).\n   655|4. **Sidecar write** (this section) — final file write of the run.\n   656|5. `final_response` summary — no further tool calls.\n   657|\n   658|If the skill is invoked standalone (no `## Run metadata` block), step 4 is skipped. Steps 1–3 and 5 still apply.\n   659|\n   660|---\n   661|\n   662|### 11.5 Skill-Level Enforcement\n   663|\n   664|Section 11 cannot enumerate output parameter names — those are declared per-skill in the capability YAML's `outputs` block and surface in each prompt's `## Output parameters` table. Each SKILL.md MUST include a short inline callout naming its output parameters, so the agent knows which keys to put in the sidecar JSON without parsing the YAML.\n   665|\n   666|Recommended SKILL.md callout shape:\n   667|\n   668|> **Step N: Emit harness outputs sidecar — apply execution-protocol.md Section 11.**\n   669|>\n   670|> **Output parameters this skill produces** (one key per row of the prompt's `## Output parameters` table):\n   671|>\n   672|> - `<param_name_1>`: <one-line description of the resolved value, e.g. \"the resolved output folder path written in earlier steps\">\n   673|> - `<param_name_2>`: ...\n   674|>\n   675|> Example sidecar contents (illustrative):\n   676|>\n   677|> ```json\n   678|> { \"<param_name_1>\": \"/abs/path/...\", \"<param_name_2>\": \"...\" }\n   679|> ```\n   680|\n   681|The callout is intentionally short. Trigger gate, mechanism, ordering, and violation rule all live in Section 11 — the SKILL.md only names the keys.\n   682|\n   683|---\n   684|\n   685|\n   686|### 11.6 Open Questions in the sidecar (universal)\n   687|\n   688|**Trigger.** This run wrote an `## Open Questions` / `## open_questions` section (any heading level, any spelling) into an artifact, AND the prompt has a `## Run metadata` block. Otherwise skip — nothing to mirror.\n   689|\n   690|**Rule.** Mirror that section into a reserved `_open_questions` key in the same sidecar JSON. This is not extra analysis: you already decided what the open questions are and wrote them down. This emits the same list in a form the orchestrator can act on.\n   691|\n   692|`_open_questions` is a RESERVED key. It is never a declared output parameter, never appears in the `## Output parameters` table, and is stripped by the harness before output resolution. Adding it never breaks propagation.\n   693|\n   694|```json\n   695|{\n   696|  \"prd_output_path\": \"/abs/path/to/artifacts/outputs/product-definition/prd\",\n   697|  \"_open_questions\": [\n   698|    {\n   699|      \"id\": \"PI-10\",\n   700|      \"question\": \"Which inventory slice does the pilot run on?\",\n   701|      \"subject\": \"scope\",\n   702|      \"why_it_matters\": \"Every downstream sizing estimate assumes one slice\",\n   703|      \"blocking\": true,\n   704|      \"assumption_if_unanswered\": \"one DTC channel with real sell-out visibility\",\n   705|      \"kind\": \"question\",\n   706|      \"audience\": \"client\",\n   707|      \"artifact_ref\": \"/abs/path/to/PRD-SPEC-....md#open-questions\",\n   708|      \"traces_to\": [\"EPIC-02\", \"FR-14\"]\n   709|    }\n   710|  ]\n   711|}\n   712|```\n   713|\n   714|**Fields.** `question` is the only required one. Emit every other field you actually have; omit what you do not. Never invent one — Zero-Invention applies here exactly as it does to the artifact.\n   715|\n   716|- `id` — your own local id (`PI-10`, `OQ-03`) if the section has one.\n   717|- `kind` — `question` (default) or `assumption`. See the note below; getting this right decides which verbs the operator is offered.\n   718|- `subject` — what the question is ABOUT (`scope`, `compliance`, `personas`). This is what turns a 60-row inbox into a dozen themes.\n   719|- `why_it_matters` — the consequence of leaving it open.\n   720|- `blocking` — `true` ONLY if downstream work genuinely cannot proceed. See the warning below.\n   721|- `assumption_if_unanswered` — what you actually proceeded on. **Emit this whenever you proceeded at all.** Without it, any later answer must be treated as invalidating every artifact built on the gap, because there is no basis to compare against — so a stated assumption is what keeps a confirming answer from triggering pointless rework.\n   722|- `audience` — who can answer: `client`, `architect`, `product_owner`, `internal`. `client` is the only value that can send the question out of the building.\n   723|- `artifact_ref` — absolute path (optionally `#anchor`) of the artifact that raised it.\n   724|- `traces_to` — ids the question affects (`EPIC-02`, `FR-14`, `BC-09`).\n   725|\n   726|**`kind` separates a gap from a claim.** A `question` names something UNKNOWN — the operator resolves it by supplying the value, or by accepting the assumption you proceeded on. An `assumption` names something you ASSERTED and could not verify (\"Theobald is compatible with the SAP S/4HANA landscape\", \"Fabric is licensable in Germany West Central\") — the operator resolves it by confirming it, which changes nothing because the artifact already says it, or by correcting it, which invalidates everything built on it. Emit `assumption` for anything you would have written into an `assumptions_to_validate` table. A question phrased as a statement of absence (\"No client-approved RPO exists\") is still a `question`: it needs a value supplied, not a claim confirmed. For an `assumption` you may omit `assumption_if_unanswered` — the claim IS what the work proceeded on.\n   727|\n   728|**`blocking` means the SCHEDULE, not the document.** Registry tables use `blocking: yes` to mean \"this document cannot be considered complete.\" Here it means \"downstream steps must not run until this is answered,\" and an operator has to confirm before it stops anything. If work can proceed on a stated assumption, it is `false` with an `assumption_if_unanswered` — that is the normal case.\n   729|\n   730|**Completeness.** The sidecar is complete by contract: list EVERY open question this run is raising, or omit `_open_questions` entirely. Do not emit a partial list — the orchestrator reads an unlisted question as one you resolved and stops tracking it.\n   731|\n   732|**Wording.** Copy `question` verbatim from the section you wrote. Identity is derived from the question text, so a reworded sidecar entry becomes a SECOND record instead of enriching the one already tracked.\n   733|\n   734|**Ordering.** No change to §11.4 — `_open_questions` is one more key in the same JSON, written at the same point.\n   735|\n   736|**Standalone runs.** No `## Run metadata` block means no sidecar at all (§11 trigger gate). The artifact still carries the section; nothing is lost.\n   737|\n   738|---\n</file>","isError":false}}
